# CHANGELOG lines that claim a speed and that no benchmark case measures, each with the reason (scripts/check-bench-cases.sh).
# Format: a phrase of the line (it must stay in the Unreleased section), a tab, the reason. A line that a case measures needs no entry here (its row of bench/cases.tsv names it);
# an entry whose phrase matches no claim, or that has no reason, fails the check. Short on purpose: a speed that can be measured gets a case, not a line here.
a run of a correlated `LIMIT 1` child per distinct input is priced at half	a change of the cost model that moves plans of the 258 captured statements; they need a mapping table and the captured corpus, which the cases of bench/cases do not carry (the timings are in the pull request)
merges the address lists of its segments by runs	an access-method scan that the planner takes for a key it expects to find early (a LIMIT) only when forced: the numbers are callgrind instruction counts and bench/col_scan.py timings (kind point_tid, query lim_grp10); the sort it removes is also in the plan of the case point_key_range
no longer scans the operator class catalog	planning instructions, measured with callgrind on EXPLAIN (one backend, 20 runs); bench cases time execution, not the planner
also keep a head (column format minor 18)	the planner does not choose the scan of a LIMIT over a column with few units apart from the fixed cost of the scan: measured with callgrind per statement (800,000 rows, one backend, 20 runs) and bench/col_scan.py kind ord (docs/results/col-run-heads.tsv); the case orders at 1,000,000 rows shows 0.38 ms with and without the runs
